          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+monads"} state monads
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/costate+comonads"} costate comonads
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/store+comonads"}> store comonads
            ) are typically discussed on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+categories"} cartesian closed categories
            — but, of course, the analogous construction exists on any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
          %p
            An immediate non-cartesian example is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+vector+spaces"} category of vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
            (and generally on a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            %em
              linear
              = succeed "-state/store" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
            merge into a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monad"} Frobenius monad
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FrobeniusStructure"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+quantum+systems"} open quantum systems
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"}> mixed states
            ,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+channels"} quantum channels
            and their
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
          %p
            Due to this fact and the general broad identification of (multiplicative)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            with a form of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            %em quantum state monad
            (cf. the similar situation for the
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+reader+monad"} quantum reader monad
          %p
            For example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+quantum+channels"} unitary quantum channels
            turn out to be embodied by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+transformations"} monad transformations
            between such quantum state monads, so that with this terminology the plausible and desireable statement “
            %em> quantum channels are quantum state transformations
            ” becomes an actual theorem (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#QuantumChannelsAreQuantumStateTransformations"} below
          %h2#preliminaries Preliminaries
          %p
            For definiteness (only), we consider the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_{\mathbb{C}}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+spaces"} complex vector spaces
            with, as usual:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal structure
                given by the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝟙
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ≡
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{1} \,\equiv\, \mathbb{C}
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
                themselves,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mo ⊸
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \multimap \mathscr{H}'
                the vector space of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
              #eq:InternalHomHomIsomorphism.maruku-equation
                %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mtext -
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mo ⊸
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mtext -
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathscr{H} \otimes (\text{-})
                      \\;\;\dashv\;\;
                      \\mathscr{H} \multimap (\text{-})
          %p Throughout the following we fix
          #eq:TheFinDimHilbertSpace.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi FinDimMod
                    %mi ℂ
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathscr{H} \,\in\, FinDimMod_{\mathbb{C}} \hookrightarrow Mod_{\mathbb{C}}
          %p
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex vector space
          %p
            But most or all of the following discussion works for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_{\mathbb{C}}
            replaced by any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+closed+category"} symmetric monoidal closed category
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
            taken from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
          %p
            In this vein, for notational purposes only (at this point) we choose on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⟨
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mo ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left\langle\cdot \vert \cdot\right\rangle
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+space"} finite-dimensional Hilbert space
                and use
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bra-ket"} bra-ket
                notation to denote the generic elements of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
                by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo |
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo ⟩
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ≡
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi ψ
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
