          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#quantum_observables_are_the_quantum_state_contextful_scalars Quantum observables are the Quantum state contextful scalars
          %blockquote
            %p (The following now starts out a little repetitive, recalling a special case of the general statement above. Will streamline…)
          %p
            For the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/costate+comonad"} costate comonad
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            its value and its operations on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            in this case) are vacuous. Quite in contrast, the linear CoState comonad on the tensor unit encodes core structure of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %p
          #QuantumObservablesAsQuantumStateContextfulScalars.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (quantum observables as quantum state contextful scalars)
              %br/
            %p First notice that the quantum store on the tensor unit
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
